# Ada-based Rust

Ada-based Rust is a deterministic Rust execution foundation with an Ada/SPARK
proof boundary. The project is being built around a small set of explicit
invariants:

- normal control flow returns `Result`; recoverable failures do not panic;
- the core does not read the wall clock, spawn implicit work, or depend on
	unordered iteration;
- failures have stable numeric codes and are written synchronously to a log;
- shutdown is explicit and reports whether the log was durably synchronized;
- the SPARK package specifies the bounded error and shutdown contract that the
	Rust implementation must satisfy.

The runtime foundation is paired with a Rust-native backend core. It is
deliberately small so that each compiler boundary can be verified before a
frontend, optimizer, and machine-code emitter are added.

## Requirements

- Rust 1.78 or newer
- Alire and a GNAT/SPARK toolchain for the proof package

The current machine has Rust and Alire, but `gnatprove` and `gprbuild` are not
currently on `PATH`. Rust tests therefore run independently of SPARK proof
analysis until the GNAT/SPARK toolchain is installed.

Provision the Ada/SPARK tools through Alire with:

```text
./scripts/setup-spark.sh
```

The setup declares GNATprove 16.1.0 and SPARK libraries 16.1.0 in
`alire.toml`. Verification can then run in the Alire environment with:

```text
alr exec -- ./scripts/verify-spark.sh
```

## Run

```text
cargo test
cargo run -- --log-path target/ada-based-rust.log
./scripts/bootstrap.sh
./scripts/bootstrap-cargo.sh
./scripts/bootstrap-pure-rust.sh
./scripts/verify-spark.sh
./scripts/setup-spark.sh
./scripts/verify-formal.sh
./scripts/verify-rust-formal.sh
./scripts/verify-llvm.sh
./scripts/verify-rust-native.sh
./scripts/verify-rust-native-pipeline.sh
```

The command exits with a nonzero status only when the runtime cannot complete
its requested operation or cannot synchronize its log. Application failures
are represented as structured errors and are not converted into panics.

Validate generated text against the installed LLVM-compatible frontend:

```text
./scripts/verify-llvm.sh
```

This uses `clang -x ir` to parse and compile the emitted IR. It is a
compatibility check against LLVM IR syntax, not proof that the Rust backend
implements all LLVM semantics.

For the complete description of the implemented compiler behavior, supported
grammar, APIs, verification tools, bootstrap modes, and current limitations,
see [MANUAL.md](MANUAL.md).

## Bootstrap

`scripts/bootstrap.sh` is the initial self-bootstrap pipeline. Stage 0 tests
and builds the source with the host Rust toolchain. Stage 1 rebuilds the same
source in an isolated target directory and executes that resulting binary and
its Rust formal checker. This proves the runtime and its bounded proof tool can
reproduce their own build pipeline; a genuinely self-hosting Ada/SPARK compiler
frontend and backend will be added later.

## Formal verification

The Ada/SPARK contract is built by
`spark/ada_based_runtime_verification.gpr` and proved by `scripts/verify-spark.sh` at
proof level 2. The proof checks the bounded sequence increment and lifecycle
transitions, including the precondition that prevents sequence overflow.

Bootstrap runs formal verification automatically when `gnatprove` and
`gprbuild` are available. To require the proof tools, use:

```text
VERIFY_SPARK=required ./scripts/bootstrap.sh
```

Without the tools, the script reports that formal verification was skipped;
it never claims that the SPARK proof succeeded.

## Multi-prover verification

The repository includes reference models for Coq, Lean, and Isabelle/HOL with
Sledgehammer in `proofs/`. Run all installed provers with:

```text
./scripts/verify-formal.sh
```

The orchestrator never claims success for a prover that is unavailable. To
make missing tools a hard failure, use:

```text
FORMAL_VERIFICATION=required ./scripts/verify-formal.sh
```

These are reference-model proofs of deterministic arithmetic properties, not
yet refinement proofs of the Rust implementation. The refinement boundary is
the next formal-methods milestone.

## Rust formal checker

`src/formal.rs` contains a dependency-free executable proof kernel for the
current expression subset. It evaluates and normalizes add/subtract/multiply
trees with checked `i64` arithmetic, then rejects normalization counterexamples.
Run it with:

```text
./scripts/verify-rust-formal.sh
```

This bounded checker is part of the Rust implementation and complements, but
does not replace, general theorem provers such as Coq, Lean, Isabelle, and
Sledgehammer.

Verify an individual expression with:

```text
cargo run --bin rust-formal-check -- --expr "2 + 3 * (5 - 1)"
```

## Pure-Rust bootstrap

`scripts/bootstrap-pure-rust.sh` does not use Cargo, LLVM bindings, or an
external Rust library. It uses the installed `rustc` only as the unavoidable
stage-0 seed, compiles the library and executable directly, rebuilds both in a
second isolated stage, and executes stage 1. Removing the seed `rustc` entirely
would require implementing a complete Rust compiler and standard library before
this project could compile itself from source.

## Cargo and crates.io

The Rust package is intentionally dependency-free and can be built using only
Cargo and the Rust standard library. `scripts/bootstrap-cargo.sh` performs a
locked two-stage Cargo build. The manifest includes crates.io metadata so the
package can be checked with:

```text
cargo package --allow-dirty --no-verify
```

`cargo package` creates a distributable source archive; publishing requires a
crates.io account and should only happen after the SPARK proof environment and
compatibility tests are available. crates.io distributes the Rust crate, but
does not replace the host `rustc` seed needed for bootstrapping.

## Layout

- `src/backend.rs`: verified deterministic SSA-like IR and LLVM-like text emitter
- `src/frontend.rs`: dependency-free integer-expression frontend to backend IR
- `src/error.rs`: stable error codes and explicit error values
- `src/log.rs`: ordered, synchronous, durable error logging
- `src/runtime.rs`: deterministic operation and explicit shutdown lifecycle
- `spark/`: Ada/SPARK contract for the Rust-facing runtime boundary
- `spark/ada_based_ir.ads`: bounded Ada/SPARK LLVM-like IR contract

## Rust-native LLVM replacement

`src/backend.rs` is the first slice of the LLVM replacement. It owns a small
SSA-like intermediate representation, verifies definitions and operands before
emission, and emits stable LLVM-like text without linking to LLVM. It now also
performs deterministic basic constant folding. The complete staged rewrite is
tracked in [LLVM_REWRITE_PLAN.md](LLVM_REWRITE_PLAN.md); this repository does
not yet claim to implement all of LLVM.

The frontend currently accepts integer expressions with `+`, `-`, `*`, and
parentheses, such as `2 + 3 * (5 - 1)`. It lowers them to verified IR and
reports malformed input through `Result` without panicking.

## Determinism boundary

The core emits an increasing sequence number for each log record. It does not
generate timestamps, use randomness, or rely on concurrency. The filesystem
is an external effect and is isolated behind the logger; failures from that
effect are returned as `Result` values. A process terminated by `SIGKILL` or a
power loss cannot guarantee a final shutdown record, but every successfully
accepted error is flushed and synchronized immediately.


